        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "e8e6b143abe6d3e5ee82f0b4952b532558537aa0",
            "parents": [
                "92b89c6ac71f8b94f67f508102704a1cf38e68cb"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/60/89660/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "rashidmp",
                "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                "username": "rashidmp2002"
            },
            "createdOn": 1777272760,
            "author": {
                "name": "rashidmp",
                "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                "username": "rashidmp2002"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "Looks good to me.\nThis is for the D-Bus layer; is there interest in adding this to the Redfish layer?\nAs a minor note: OpenBMC service-config-manager can also disable services (like IPMI on UDP port 623) which overlaps part of the firewall port blocking function."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, Redfish layer support is planned.\n\nYou're correct that service-config-manager provides per-instance service control. The firewall feature complements this by providing network-level traffic filtering that service-config-manager cannot.\n\nThe firewall can allow or block traffic based on:\n-IP addresses or subnets \n-Time windows \n-Protocol and port ranges \n-MAC addresses"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "If Redfish support is planned, please document Redfish schemas you plan to support."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Overall I think this design doc needs to be made more specific as to what use cases it's trying to implement, and tie those out with what Redfish supports.  If someone wants to configure a firewall generically, there are lots of APIs for doing that directly and in my opinion OpenBMC doesn't need to invent a new one.  If there's use cases specific to the BMC that we want to support, we might model some of those directly and specifically, but that's not what this doc does.\n\nThis is roughly the same as why we don't model a generic \"gpio\" on dbus.  We're trying to model portable and movable use cases, not just taking one API and splatting it to another."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "I hope I have addressed the comment, in the document. Kindly review the updated design document and let me know if you have any concerns."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "Let we conclude this design document first, if design is enough to do the implementation. I will propose the required changes in redfish forum."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why would anyone be doing this?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "updated problem description."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "This is not true.  Every service is expected to have a mechanism to disable that service.  If you don't want / don't trust a service to run, disable it.\n\nThat is functionally the exact same as a software firewall."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "Existing BMC configuration controls allow the BMC admin to disable network protocols (such as https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/master/REDFISH-cheatsheet.md#disable-ipmi).  This is better than blocking the network port (such as UDP port 623) because the service is not running and not taking BMC resources.\n\nHowever, a firewall provides more function than just blocking ports.  It can allow specific network clients to use specific BMC network services, and block all others.  For example, this can help prevent BMC denial of service conditions due to misconfigured BMC clients."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "You need documentation on _why_ these are good things and/or expected things for a BMC to add firewall behaviors for."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "Updated problem Description."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Need a better problem description here.  You say people are manually setting up firewall rules, but don't enumerate WHY they're doing it.\n\n> limiting BMC access to specific\nmanagement networks\n\nThis seems like a reasonable use case to support\n\n> blocking unused protocols\n\nPlease look at the ManagerNetworkProtocol class in Redfish.  It implements runtime support for disabling unused protocols.\n\n\n> implementing time-based access controls for maintenance windows.\n\nWould the \"time-based\" access generally be managed by a BMC?  Normally that would be managed by an external controller doing the update."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "ManagerNetworkProtocol and firewall serve different purposes:\nManagerNetworkProtocol: Disables services globally (e.g., \"turn off SSH completely\")\nFirewall: Controls network access per source (e.g., \"allow SSH only from specific IP\" or \"block all UDP from specific IPs\")\nThey're complementary - one controls which services run, the other controls who can access them. That said, IP-based filtering is the primary use case.\n\nThe implementation supports time-based rules (using iptables' time module) for scenarios where rules should automatically activate/deactivate at specific times without external intervention."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 63,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Please rewrite as actual use cases.  \"reorder (all) firewall rules\" is overly broad and not generally how we organize dbus.  Also, how (at a requirements level) do you expect users to use these if they're only exposed to DBus?  Only on dbus would mean that we're still just executing shell scripts, at which point, we haven't really replaced nftables commands, right?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 63,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "The D-Bus interface is consumed by bmcweb/Redfish, not directly by users. End users interact via Redfish API."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This already exists.  Services only bind on the protocols they support.  We likely need to define a way to block ICMP ping (as that's handled by the kernel), but that's just another service we add to the list."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "The protocol filtering can be source-based - we can block or accept protocols based on the source IP/network or MAC address. \n\nExamples:\n-Block TCP protocol entirely\n-Allow TCP from 192.168.1.0/24 but block TCP from other networks\n-Allow SSH only from MAC address aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff\n-Block ICMP from specific source IPs"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 69,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "?????  What would this mean?  Port filtering only makes sense in gateways, where sockets are being forwarded through to another entity.  the BMC doesn't forward arbitrary ranges of sockets to other devices.  If that's what you're doing, that needs added to the background that you're using the BMC as a nat/non nat gateway, which likely significantly changes this doc."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 69,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "This is input filtering for traffic destined to the BMC itself, not port forwarding/NAT.\nThese are standard iptables -A INPUT rules with -p tcp --dport <port> or --dport <start>:<end> for ranges. The filtering happens on traffic coming into the BMC, before it reaches the listening services."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 116,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "How would this work in Redfish?  This \"firewall\" feature has been proposed multiple times at a DMTF standards level, and every time ran into the problem of not being able to define the use case (same as above) well enough to even come up with a model, or why it's separate from the things that already exist.\n\n\nIs there more details there on what the Redfish API looks like somewhere?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 116,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "Let we conclude this design document first, if design is enough to do the implementation. I will propose the required changes in redfish forum."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 136,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm really hesitant to write yet-another-iptables-manipulator.  Aren't there 100 existing open-source implementations of this?  Please document alternatives and why those are not sufficient."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 136,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "+1"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 136,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
                        "username": "rashidmp2002"
                    },
                    "message": "Updated Alternatives Considered."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 159,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "You didn't follow the openbmc design document template.  Please fill out all sections, especially \"Alternatives\"."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/firewall-configuration.md",
                    "line": 159,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rashidmp",
                        "email": "rashidmp@ami.com",
